### C Function to Reverse a String In-Place

The core idea is to use two pointers (one at the beginning and one at the end) and swap the characters they point to, moving them towards the center until they meet or cross.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

/**
 * reverseStringInPlace
 * 
 * Reverses a null-terminated string in place.
 * 
 * @param str A pointer to the null-terminated string to be reversed.
 */
void reverseStringInPlace(char *str) {
    if (str == NULL) {
        return; // Handle the case where the input pointer is NULL
    }

    // 1. Initialize pointers
    int start = 0;
    // Calculate the length of the string (excluding the null terminator)
    int length = strlen(str);
    int end = length - 1;

    // 2. Loop until the pointers meet in the middle
    while (start < end) {
        // Swap the characters at the start and end positions
        char temp = str[start];
        str[start] = str[end];
        str[end] = temp;

        // Move the pointers inward
        start++;
        end--;
    }
}

// --- Example Usage ---
int main() {
    // Test Case 1: Standard string
    char str1[] = "hello world";
    printf("Original string 1: %s\n", str1);
    reverseStringInPlace(str1);
    printf("Reversed string 1: %s\n\n", str1);

    // Test Case 2: Palindrome (should look the same)
    char str2[] = "madam";
    printf("Original string 2: %s\n", str2);
    reverseStringInPlace(str2);
    printf("Reversed string 2: %s\n\n", str2);

